@implements IDisposable
@inject IDialogService Dialogs
@* Written in each component, not in an _Imports.razor, whose class Razor makes public (ADR-0201).
   This project's root namespace is ExSheet.MudBlazor, so a plain "MudBlazor" here is this
   namespace, not MudBlazor's, and every product using is written with global:: (the name
   collisions in AGENTS.md). HorizontalAlignment is both MudBlazor's and the engine's; here it is
   the engine's, the one a Cell Format holds. *@
@using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Components
@using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Components.Web
@using global::MudBlazor
@using global::MudBlazor.Utilities
@using global::ExSheet
@using global::ExSheet.Engine
@using HorizontalAlignment = global::ExSheet.Engine.HorizontalAlignment
@* Not the contract (ADR-0201): MudSheetChrome renders this for Format Cells, and no Consumer places
   it. It is public only because Razor makes every component public, is hidden from a Consumer's
   editor, and may change in any version. *@
@attribute [System.ComponentModel.EditorBrowsable(System.ComponentModel.EditorBrowsableState.Never)]

@*
    Where ExSheet renders the MudBlazor Chrome's Format Cells (ADR-0071, ADR-0010's note of
    2026-09-30): beside the grid, outside its root, from the opening until OK or Cancel. It shows
    MudFormatCellsDialog through MudBlazor's dialog service, at page level, and draws nothing of
    its own but the place the keyboard leaves from.

    That place is the one subtle part. MudBlazor's dialog hands the keyboard, on closing, back to
    whatever held it when the dialog opened. Opened from a page's button, that is the button, and
    the core's focus function, granted only while DOM focus is inside the grid or on nothing
    (ADR-0021's note of 2026-09-30), would then leave the keyboard on the button: Format Cells
    closed and the next arrow went nowhere. So the keyboard is first put on this frame's own empty
    element, which the dialog then takes it from; the element goes once the dialog has the
    keyboard, so what the dialog hands it back to on closing is no longer on the page, and DOM
    focus is on nothing. No script is added: the element is focused with Blazor's own FocusAsync
    (ADR-0021). It is fixed to the viewport, so that neither focusing it nor handing the keyboard
    back to it scrolls the page.

    The core's focus function is called once the dialog has left the page, however it closed, and
    decides as it does for every Chrome. "Left the page" is in the browser, not on the server: on a
    circuit a request sent behind the dialog's removal still reached the browser before it, while
    the keyboard was inside the dialog, and was declined (layer 3, Server: six in ten). So the
    dialog closes itself first — on OK too, unless the draft holds a refusal — and this frame draws
    once more and waits for that render's OnAfterRenderAsync, which Blazor runs once the browser
    has applied the render, the dialog's removal with it. Only then does it set OK's change or the
    Cancel, and hand the keyboard back.

    The element holds the keys typed while the keyboard waits on it (KeysOnTheirWay): on a circuit
    the dialog is drawn, and its tabs take the keyboard, round trips after the gesture, and an End
    typed as the dialog appeared was lost (layer 3, CI). They reach the tab shown once the tabs hold
    the keyboard. Their defaults are the element's, prevented: an End there would scroll the page.
    It is never aria-hidden: it holds focus, and Chromium refuses to hide a focused element, saying
    so in the console while its accessibility tree is on (the seventeenth Windows run, Part D).

    Closing from the keys — Escape, OK, Cancel — is the keyboard in flight back to the Sheet, and the
    keys typed meanwhile are the Sheet's, in order (decided with the user, 2026-10-13). On a circuit
    the dialog stands a round trip after the key that closes it, and once MudBlazor removes it DOM
    focus is on nothing, where a key reaches no handler at all: ↓ typed straight after Escape was
    lost. So the close goes the other way round. OK sets what was touched at once, as it is pressed, so
    that nothing typed after it changes what it sets, and Cancel ends Format Cells; ExSheet keeps this
    frame on the page until the keyboard has left it (FormatCellsContext.ReturnKeyboardFrom). The
    element comes back, takes the keyboard out of the dialog, and only then does the dialog close.
    The keys typed in the dialog from the closing key on (the dialog passes them here) and on the
    element are held, and the core takes the keyboard back from the element with them, handing them
    to the grid ahead of any typed after (ADR-0050 item 16's note of 2026-10-12). Closed any other way
    — the ×, the backdrop — it goes as it always has.
*@
@if (_leaving)
{
    <span class="mud-ex-sheet-format-cells-leaving" style="position: fixed; top: 0; left: 0" tabindex="-1"
          @onkeydown="_keyOnTheWay" @onkeydown:preventDefault @ref="_leavingFrom"></span>
}

@code {
    // The dialog is modal, closes on Escape (its own, through this frame) and on a press on its
    // backdrop, and has its × — each a Cancel. MudFormatCellsDialog takes the keyboard to the tab shown itself, and tells MudBlazor's
    // focus trap to take no first focus of its own, which would land before the tabs are drawn:
    // MudBlazor 9 reads that from the dialog, not from these options. It is as wide on every tab.
    private static readonly DialogOptions Options = new()
    {
        // Escape is the dialog's own, through this frame (MudFormatCellsDialog.Closing).
        CloseOnEscapeKey = false,
        BackdropClick = true,
        CloseButton = true,
        MaxWidth = MaxWidth.Small,
        FullWidth = true,
    };

    // Whether the element the keyboard leaves from stands, and that element; and the keys typed
    // on it, on their way to the dialog.
    private bool _leaving = true;
    private ElementReference _leavingFrom;
    private readonly KeysOnTheirWay _keys = new();
    private readonly Func<KeyboardEventArgs, Task> _keyOnTheWay;
    private readonly Func<Func<KeyboardEventArgs, Task<bool>>, Task> _keysArrive;
    // ExSheet renders this frame whenever it renders, the grid's scrolling included; it draws
    // again only when the element goes (ADR-0003's rule: skip what has not changed).
    private bool _changed;
    private bool _disposed;
    private IDialogReference? _dialog;
    private readonly TaskCompletionSource _drawn = new(TaskCreationOptions.RunContinuationsAsynchronously);
    // Closing from the keys: the keys typed from the closing key on, in the dialog and on the element,
    // held for the Sheet until the core asks for them; whether OK closed it; and whether the element is
    // to take the keyboard, and the dialog then close, after the render that draws it.
    private List<KeyboardEventArgs>? _closingKeys;
    private bool _closingOk;
    private bool _leaveThenClose;
    private readonly Func<bool, Task> _closing;
    private readonly Action<KeyboardEventArgs> _keyWhileClosing;
    private readonly Func<IReadOnlyList<KeyboardEventArgs>> _takeClosingKeys;
    // Completed by the render that follows the dialog's closing, once the browser has drawn it.
    private TaskCompletionSource? _drawnAfterClosing;

    /// <summary>ExSheet's Format Cells, as it opened (ADR-0071).</summary>
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public FormatCellsContext Context { get; set; } = default!;

    /// <summary>Where the keys typed on the element go, held in a field (AGENTS.md, the Blazor traps).</summary>
    public MudFormatCellsFrame()
    {
        _keyOnTheWay = OnElementKeyAsync;
        _keysArrive = _keys.ArrivedAsync;
        _closing = CloseFromKeysAsync;
        _keyWhileClosing = key => _closingKeys?.Add(key);
        _takeClosingKeys = TakeClosingKeys;
    }

    // A key on the element: on the way into the dialog, or out of it.
    private Task OnElementKeyAsync(KeyboardEventArgs key)
    {
        if (_closingKeys is { } closing)
        {
            closing.Add(key);
            return Task.CompletedTask;
        }
        return _keys.TypedAsync(key);
    }

    // Escape, OK or Cancel in the dialog: OK sets what was touched now, or Cancel ends Format Cells, and
    // the element takes the keyboard out of the dialog before it closes (decided with the user,
    // 2026-10-13). Without the core's hand-back from a frame, the dialog closes as it always has.
    private async Task CloseFromKeysAsync(bool ok)
    {
        if (_closingKeys is not null || _disposed) return;
        if (Context.ReturnKeyboardFrom is null)
        {
            _dialog?.Close(ok ? DialogResult.Ok(true) : DialogResult.Cancel());
            return;
        }
        _closingKeys = [];
        _closingOk = ok;
        if (ok)
        {
            if (!await Context.Ok())
            {
                // A refusal the dialog did not see: Format Cells stays, and so does the dialog.
                _closingKeys = null;
                return;
            }
        }
        else
        {
            Context.Cancel();
        }
        _leaving = true;
        _leaveThenClose = true;
        _changed = true;
        StateHasChanged();
    }

    // The core has taken the keyboard back from the element: the keys held on the way are the grid's.
    private IReadOnlyList<KeyboardEventArgs> TakeClosingKeys()
    {
        var keys = _closingKeys ?? [];
        _closingKeys = [];
        return keys;
    }

    /// <inheritdoc />
    protected override bool ShouldRender()
    {
        if (!_changed) return false;
        _changed = false;
        return true;
    }

    /// <inheritdoc />
    protected override async Task OnAfterRenderAsync(bool firstRender)
    {
        if (!firstRender)
        {
            if (_leaveThenClose)
            {
                _leaveThenClose = false;
                await LeaveThenCloseAsync();
                return;
            }
            _drawnAfterClosing?.TrySetResult();
            return;
        }
        try
        {
            await _leavingFrom.FocusAsync(preventScroll: true);
        }
        catch (Exception ex) when (ex is Microsoft.JSInterop.JSException or Microsoft.JSInterop.JSDisconnectedException or ObjectDisposedException or OperationCanceledException)
        {
            // The circuit went, or ExSheet ended Format Cells, before the focus landed.
        }
        if (_disposed) return;
        var parameters = new DialogParameters<MudFormatCellsDialog>
        {
            { d => d.Context, Context },
            { d => d.KeyboardTaken, (Action)KeyboardTaken },
            { d => d.Drawn, (Action)(() => _drawn.TrySetResult()) },
            { d => d.KeysArrive, _keysArrive },
            { d => d.Closing, _closing },
            { d => d.KeyWhileClosing, _keyWhileClosing },
        };
        var dialog = await Dialogs.ShowAsync<MudFormatCellsDialog>(MudFormatCellsDialog.Title, parameters, Options);
        if (_disposed)
        {
            // ExSheet ended this opening while the dialog was being shown.
            dialog.Close(DialogResult.Cancel());
            return;
        }
        _dialog = dialog;
        // MudBlazor waits five seconds for a provider to draw the dialog, then answers as if one
        // had; and it can answer a render early, when a dialog closing makes way for this one. A
        // dialog not drawn a second later was drawn by none: say so, rather than leave Format
        // Cells open where nothing shows it.
        if (!_drawn.Task.IsCompleted) await Task.WhenAny(_drawn.Task, Task.Delay(TimeSpan.FromSeconds(1)));
        if (!_drawn.Task.IsCompleted && !dialog.Result.IsCompleted)
        {
            Context.Cancel();
            // The keyboard was never taken: given back, so ExSheet lets this frame go.
            await Context.ReturnKeyboard();
            throw new InvalidOperationException(
                "Format Cells under MudSheetChrome is a MudDialog, and no MudDialogProvider drew it. Add <MudDialogProvider /> to the layout, beside <MudPopoverProvider />.");
        }
        await EndedAsync(dialog);
    }

    // The element is drawn again: it takes the keyboard out of the dialog, the dialog closes, and the core
    // takes the keyboard back from the element with the keys held on the way. ExSheet then lets this
    // frame go.
    private async Task LeaveThenCloseAsync()
    {
        try
        {
            await _leavingFrom.FocusAsync(preventScroll: true);
        }
        catch (Exception ex) when (ex is Microsoft.JSInterop.JSException or Microsoft.JSInterop.JSDisconnectedException or ObjectDisposedException or OperationCanceledException)
        {
            // The circuit went, or the element did.
        }
        _dialog?.Close(_closingOk ? DialogResult.Ok(true) : DialogResult.Cancel());
        await Context.ReturnKeyboardFrom!(_leavingFrom, _takeClosingKeys);
    }

    // The dialog has the keyboard: the element it would hand it back to goes.
    private void KeyboardTaken()
    {
        if (!_leaving || _disposed) return;
        _leaving = false;
        _changed = true;
        StateHasChanged();
    }

    // However the dialog closed. The yield puts what follows behind the render in which the
    // provider removes the dialog; then this frame draws, and waits until the browser has drawn it
    // too. OK sets what was touched (the dialog closes on OK only when the draft holds no
    // refusal); anything else is a Cancel, which sets nothing, and which ExSheet ignores once this
    // opening has ended another way. The keyboard is handed back last.
    private async Task EndedAsync(IDialogReference dialog)
    {
        var result = await dialog.Result;
        // Closed from the keys: OK or Cancel was set as it was pressed, and the keyboard goes back from
        // the element (CloseFromKeysAsync).
        if (_closingKeys is not null) return;
        await Task.Yield();
        if (!_disposed)
        {
            var drawn = _drawnAfterClosing = new TaskCompletionSource(TaskCreationOptions.RunContinuationsAsynchronously);
            _changed = true;
            StateHasChanged();
            await drawn.Task;
        }
        if (result is { Canceled: false, Data: true }) await Context.Ok();
        else Context.Cancel();
        await Context.ReturnKeyboard();
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// ExSheet ended Format Cells — by OK or Cancel, by the Selection moving under it, by a new
    /// opening — and the dialog closes with it, as a Cancel.
    /// </summary>
    public void Dispose()
    {
        _disposed = true;
        if (_dialog is { Result.IsCompleted: false } dialog) dialog.Close(DialogResult.Cancel());
        _drawnAfterClosing?.TrySetResult();
    }
}
